<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vakpatiraja (poet)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vakpatiraja was a poet of the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yashovarman-of-kannauj">Yashovarman of Kannauj</a> in the 8th century, writing in Maharashtri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudavaho">Gaudavaho</a> ('The Slaying of the King of Gauda') celebrates Yashovarman's campaigns, including the defeat of a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> and Gauda. The poem is one of the main sources for Yashovarman's reign, and Vakpatiraja names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhavabhuti">Bhavabhuti</a> as his model.</p></div>
